c2 — Ritchie's c2 peephole optimizer, modernized
=================================================

This is V7's `cmd/c/c2` (c20.c + c21.c + c2.h), run through knr2c99.py to
C99 (int16_t, prototypes) and committed in stages.  It takes PDP-11 assembler
text on stdin (or a file) and emits optimized assembler on stdout.

No adaptation to pcc's output is needed.  pcc emits the same `jbr`/`jeq`/
`jne`/... branch mnemonics that V7's own c1 emitted, and V7 as relaxes them
natively: in as26.s the opl35/opl36 ("jump/branch type", opcode 035/036)
handler converts a far branch to `jmp` (or an inverted-condition branch +
`jmp` for a conditional).  c2's optab is the original V7 table, which reads
those j-mnemonics, so it passes them through unchanged while doing its normal
peephole work (jump-to-jump elimination, literal elimination, etc.).

The full cross-compile pipeline is:

    pcc -S foo.c -o foo.s    # emit PDP-11 assembly
    c2 foo.s foo.opt.s      # peephole optimization (optional)
    as -u foo.opt.s          # assemble -> a.out (relaxes far branches)
    ld ...                   # link

c2 is optional: `pcc -S` output also assembles directly with `as -u`.

The `-u` flag to as is **required**: pcc references libc symbols (`_printf`,
`__iob`, ...) with no `.globl`, and V7 as treats an undeclared undefined as a
*local* undefined (error 'u').  `-u` (V7's `as` maps it to `defund=040`)
defaults them to external.  This is exactly how V7's own `cc` driver invokes
`as` — its `assemble:` block is `as -u -o out in` unconditionally — so the
V7 makefiles already carry the flag.
